WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:07.840
Japan is a land of tradition. It's a country where&nbsp;
ancient customs are revered. But Japan also has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.840
a long history of embracing cutting-edge&nbsp;
ideas and technologies. Like toilets that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:20.200
shoot water directly at your scrotum and&nbsp;
vending machines that sell… well, yeah.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.680
That's a bit of a contradiction, isn't it? How&nbsp;
can a country that's so deeply rooted in tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:30.200
be one of the most technologically advanced on&nbsp;
earth? Well, the truth is that Japan's evolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:36.160
from a feudal society, as recently as the 19th&nbsp;
century, to a global economic powerhouse come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.600
the 20th century, is actually one of the&nbsp;
most remarkable stories in human history.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.440
If you need an example of how eagerly&nbsp;
Japan can embrace a technology when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:52.080
wants to - look no further than the digital&nbsp;
camera, although an American invention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.840
Japan quickly “snap”-ped it up and monopolised the&nbsp;
market the likes of which I don’t think I’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.480
in any other consumer tech product.&nbsp;
Sony - Japanese, Canon - Japanese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.840
Nikon - Japanese, Fujifilm - Japanese,&nbsp;
Panasonic - Japanese, Olympus - Japanese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:18.680
Pentax - Japanese. Anyway, you get the picture&nbsp;
- sorry. Although, arguably Japan’s technologic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:25.480
export game peaked in 1958 when they invented&nbsp;
instant ramen. Oodles of noodles, in a cup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:31.200
in an instant … screw the digital camera, that’s&nbsp;
the pinnacle of human achievement right there.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:38.040
And all this from Japan. A country that only&nbsp;
opened its doors to the outside world in 1853,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:44.880
at about the same time steam trains were first&nbsp;
gaining traction in Britain. Just a quick warning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.560
that this is going to be a difficult video for me,&nbsp;
it’s all about a technology that we invented then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:58.440
decided to be so crap at it come the 21st century&nbsp;
that almost everyone else now does it better.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:03.600
Anyway, what was it that suddenly catapulted&nbsp;
the tiny island nation of Japan from a feudal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:12.960
society to a technological superpower, seemingly&nbsp;
overnight? The answer is simple: a revolution. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.240
revolution catalysed by a single technology&nbsp;
that changed everything. A technology that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.600
in the space of a few decades, broke&nbsp;
down seemingly insurmountable barriers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:31.960
and connected what were previously out-of-reach&nbsp;
intellectual minds and financial hubs. The result?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:38.440
Japan exploded, humbly making a mockery&nbsp;
of the rest of the world in the process.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:47.400
This is the fascinating story of the&nbsp;
Shinkansen - the Japanese bullet train.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:53.920
The opening of Japan's first railway line in&nbsp;
1872 represented a welcome sign of progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:58.600
but the trains were slow and unreliable,&nbsp;
and the steam engine frequently broke down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.720
leaving passengers stranded for hours at a time.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.120
As Japan grew in the first half of the 20th&nbsp;
century, more and more railway lines were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.280
built. But the issues that plagued the first&nbsp;
trains stuck around. By the 1950s, the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:17.560
speed of a Japanese train was a paltry 36 miles&nbsp;
an hour, and it wasn't uncommon for a trip from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:24.840
Tokyo to Osaka - a distance of just 300 miles - to&nbsp;
take over 7 hours. And the infrastructure made no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:30.120
sense either - tracks often followed the winding&nbsp;
course of rivers rather than the shortest distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:37.360
between two cities, meaning trains were constantly&nbsp;
slowing down to weave their way around bends.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.520
By this point, the first cars and&nbsp;
aeroplanes had already arrived in Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:47.520
and overnight the populace came to favour&nbsp;
them over sluggish trains. However, in 1958,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.080
the country was preparing to host the Olympic&nbsp;
games and Japan was keen to show the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.640
that it had evolved significantly since the&nbsp;
end of the Second World War and was now a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.640
modern nation capable of hosting the most&nbsp;
prestigious sporting event on the planet.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:07.800
But their embarrassing rail system didn’t&nbsp;
exactly shout “progress”, so in 1959,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.640
the Japanese government solidified plans for the&nbsp;
first ever high-speed railway line. The project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:19.040
would cost more than Jeff Bezos’ divorce&nbsp;
- actually nothing costs that much - but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.720
at an estimated $200 billion Yen (about £1&nbsp;
billion), it would be pricey. However the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.920
government was willing to spend big. This was&nbsp;
about more than just getting people from A to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:35.440
B - this was a statement - a reaffirmation to&nbsp;
the world that, yes, Japan had been defeated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:44.960
but was now ready to rise again from the ashes&nbsp;
as a phoenix, a sexy metal train-shaped phoenix.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.960
But to pull this off the Shinkansen engineers&nbsp;
would have to innovate in quite a few ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:54.880
For a start, unlike other train systems, which&nbsp;
often shared tracks with slower local trains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.040
the Shinkansen would operate on its own&nbsp;
dedicated routes. Moreover, these tracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.960
were engineered with banked turns, allowing the&nbsp;
train to maintain its blistering speeds even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:10.280
through curves. They weren't building a railway,&nbsp;
they were building a Goddamn rollercoaster.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.720
To enhance the smoothness of the ride, the&nbsp;
tracks employed continuous welded rails.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.840
Traditionally tracks were short segments bolted&nbsp;
together, which incidentally is the primary cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:26.400
of the all-too-familiar "click-clack" noise. The&nbsp;
Shinkansen's seamless tracks meant less friction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:32.440
which meant higher speeds and less maintenance.&nbsp;
And when your entire country is prone to sporadic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:39.560
Godzilla attacks, having to fix stuff less often&nbsp;
is a Godsend. To ensure the Shinkansen would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.400
stable at such high speeds it was necessary&nbsp;
for the existing Cape Gauge tracks (at 3ft 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.640
inches wide) to be beefed up to what we now call&nbsp;
“Standard” Gauge (at a girthy 4 ft 8.5 inches).

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.280
Level crossings were eliminated too&nbsp;
- bridges were utilised frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:58.440
along the routes so cars don’t have to&nbsp;
slow down for trains and vice-versa.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:03.800
Rather than coil springs the Shinkansen used a&nbsp;
revolutionary air-spring suspension system to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:09.200
increase comfort and stability. And, of course,&nbsp;
the Shinkansen was given an iconically massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:15.760
pointy nose. It looked like pure science-fiction&nbsp;
in the 60s, and it was a hallmark of aerodynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:20.760
design, meticulously crafted to minimise&nbsp;
air resistance and to suppress “tunnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:27.280
boom” - the loud booming noise created when&nbsp;
a train enters a tunnel at high velocity.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:30.120
They even created a special diagnostics train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.760
nicknamed “Doctor Yellow” - this bright yellow&nbsp;
rolling stock is equipped with special sensors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.320
to monitor the condition of the tracks&nbsp;
below and the overhead wires. All so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.720
faults in the network can be detected&nbsp;
as quickly and efficiently as possible.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.040
And all these efforts paid off.&nbsp;
The fastest steam train in Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.920
at the time topped out at 60 mph, and&nbsp;
the first Shinkansen railway - now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:07:00.760
known as the Tokaido line - had a top&nbsp;
speed of 130 mph, more than double.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.120
That may not sound particularly fast by today's&nbsp;
standards, but it was a game changer for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:11.720
people of Japan. It slashed the average journey&nbsp;
time from Tokyo to Osaka to just 4 hours. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:16.160
got a train that takes longer than that in&nbsp;
England and it only goes to fucking Wigan.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.680
Then in 1975 the Sanyo Shinkansen extension&nbsp;
line was built, connecting Tokyo all the way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:29.000
Fukuoka - you could now go from Fucking Fukuoka to&nbsp;
Tokyo - a distance of 680 miles, in just 5 hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:33.640
without changing trains - the people of&nbsp;
Fukuoka thought this was Fuckoak-ing brilliant.&nbsp;

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:37.960
The success of the Shinkansen wasn't just due&nbsp;
to the massive reduction in journey times. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:43.840
were also remarkably reliable - something that&nbsp;
couldn't be said for their conventional cousins.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.400
A railway line is like a choose your own adventure&nbsp;
book - every few miles there's a point where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:52.720
trains can go one way or the other. And, just&nbsp;
like in a choose your own adventure book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:55.960
making the wrong choice can be the&nbsp;
difference between getting to work on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:01.320
time or ending up naked in a swamp with&nbsp;
your ear being mauled by a horny otter.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:05.720
If a train passes a junction and follows the&nbsp;
wrong track, the driver can't simply pull a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:10.280
U-turn. The train will have to keep going until&nbsp;
it reaches the next station, at which point it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:13.080
change tracks and go back the way it came. These&nbsp;
“schedule deviations” can add hours to a journey.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.560
In order to avoid schedule deviations, which&nbsp;
were plaguing conventional train lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:22.200
the Shinkansen were equipped with an automated&nbsp;
system called Centralised Traffic Control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.960
that allowed them to seamlessly navigate&nbsp;
even the most complex junctions without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.960
having to slow down. Together with&nbsp;
a second automated safety system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:36.600
called Automatic Train Control (ATC),&nbsp;
also developed for the Shinkansen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:40.880
the system could automatically slow down any&nbsp;
train on the network depending on the speed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:47.080
the trains ahead or behind it and intervene if&nbsp;
a driver fails to respond to hazards in time.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:52.760
Contrary to popular belief, the original&nbsp;
Shinkansen trains never actually levitated. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:58.040
requires something called maglev technology, which&nbsp;
has only been employed in real-life sporadically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:03.560
over the years, usually for very short journeys,&nbsp;
like airport shuttles. Most notably the Shanghai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:11.880
maglev was opened in 2004, offering top speeds&nbsp;
of 270 mph. Although the Shanghai maglev is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:17.480
completely bloody useless - it only covers a&nbsp;
distance of 19 miles, over 7 and a half minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:22.800
which means by the time it reaches full speed&nbsp;
in the middle of its journey, it has to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:29.720
slowing down again. As a result it averages&nbsp;
a speed of 155 mph, slower than a non-maglev&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:35.760
high-speed train. I guess this is what happens&nbsp;
when a country has too much disposable income.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:42.600
Despite not levitating, the original Shinkansen&nbsp;
trains were designed specifically to minimise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.880
friction and vibration in every way. So they&nbsp;
could glide along the tracks like Usain Bolt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:54.120
with a greased up arse. Even though the trains&nbsp;
were travelling at 130 mph, you could balance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.880
a coin on its edge and it wouldn't fall over.&nbsp;
Passengers could place cups of tea on the window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:04.400
ledges in the knowledge they wouldn't spill.&nbsp;
This might seem rather mundane to us today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:10.240
but back in the 60s, it was as if the&nbsp;
laws of physics had been rewritten.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:16.000
Construction of the Shinkansen network was rapid.&nbsp;
Now there are 9 individual but interconnected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.440
shinkansen lines, and counting. Connecting the&nbsp;
entire country, all the way from Sapporo in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:29.040
North, to Kagoshima in the South. With the fastest&nbsp;
train in Japan now cruising at a speedy 200 mph.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:34.160
Today, the average delay for a&nbsp;
Shinkansen train is less than one minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:39.560
and that includes delays caused by natural&nbsp;
disasters! To put that in perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:44.160
a British train being delayed by less&nbsp;
than one minute is rarer than seeing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:49.000
unicorn shitting gold coins, inside&nbsp;
a Wetherspoons. And that’s because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:53.240
our trains are so old that the tracks are&nbsp;
made from the bones of Victorian children.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.800
Speaking of natural disasters - Shinkansen&nbsp;
lines are fitted with earthquake detection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:00.360
systems which can automatically cut&nbsp;
power to trains if early warning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:05.760
signs are detected of a potentially dangerous&nbsp;
earthquake. Which is nice to know, I guess.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:09.800
The implications of the Shinkansen&nbsp;
went beyond convenience. Economically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:15.240
cities that were connected by the Shinkansen&nbsp;
experienced unprecedented growth, from Osaka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:20.720
to Nagoya to Hiroshima. It was like a game&nbsp;
of Monopoly where everyone wins and you don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.800
spend four hours threatening to murder&nbsp;
your family for not selling you Mayfair.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:29.160
Furthermore, the Shinkansen revolutionised the way&nbsp;
people lived and worked. Families no longer had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:33.760
to be uprooted for job opportunities. Someone&nbsp;
could work in Tokyo but choose to live in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:40.560
quieter city like Shizuoka and commute daily. Such&nbsp;
a concept was unthinkable before the Shinkansen.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.560
But the effects rippled around the globe.&nbsp;
Japan took a billion-pound-gamble and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:50.960
pretty much placed it all on a single&nbsp;
train system. And boy did it pay off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:56.640
transforming it from a broken post-war economy to&nbsp;
being the third richest country on the freaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:02.920
planet. And that’s enough to make all the other&nbsp;
countries stand up and take notice. The Shinkansen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:08.480
success story inspired the construction of similar&nbsp;
high-speed rail systems like the TGV in France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:14.960
the AVE in Spain, Germany’s ICE and many&nbsp;
more three-letter-acronym-inclined-nations.

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:22.480
But it can be argued that none have managed to&nbsp;
emulate the success of the Shinkansen. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:27.080
there are now faster trains out there.&nbsp;
But speed was never the primary goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:32.280
of Shinkansen’s design - it was capacity.&nbsp;
It was designed to move large numbers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:40.240
people quickly - this is a country with 125&nbsp;
million people and the world’s largest city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:47.640
And today during peak times a high speed&nbsp;
train departs from Tokyo every 3 minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:52.520
Every single day about a quarter of a million&nbsp;
people ride the Shinkansen in Japan. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:57.840
after 50 years of operation and over 10&nbsp;
billion passengers, the Shinkansen has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:04.720
never had a single fatal accident - officially&nbsp;
making it the world’s safest train system.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:10.680
And now, Japan is looking to the future, as&nbsp;
they always have done. New lines are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:16.320
developed using superconducting maglev technology&nbsp;
- like in Shanghai - which promises speeds of up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:24.880
to 310 mph. When complete the Chūō Shinkansen&nbsp;
will be the longest maglev route in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:31.240
and a trip from Tokyo to Osaka will&nbsp;
take a breathtaking 67 minutes.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:36.840
The primary driving force behind the inception&nbsp;
of the first Shinkansen was a humble engineer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:42.840
Hideo Shima, an employee of Japanese National&nbsp;
Railways, who was intensely passionate about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:48.760
the future of trains and their core role&nbsp;
in public mass transit - he had a vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:55.560
and fortunately his government listened. So,&nbsp;
you see, the Shinkansen was never just a train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:02.480
and it was never about speed, or world-leading&nbsp;
safety, or having shiny new toys to show off. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:10.520
was a symbol of Japan's resilience, innovation,&nbsp;
and vision. It was about saying - this is what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:14.720
possible when government funds are put&nbsp;
into projects that make sense for and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:21.920
directly benefit the people, rather than vanity&nbsp;
projects designed to win elections. Personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:27.920
I think most countries can learn a lesson or&nbsp;
two from this humble electric train. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:37.800
the ‘Bullet Train’ well and truly shot&nbsp;
Japan into the 21st century and beyond.

